WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.668
-Our next guest is the mayor
of New York City!

00:00:02.668 --> 00:00:05.705
Please welcome to the show
Mayor Eric Adams.

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:07.573
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:16.315
♪♪

00:00:16.315 --> 00:00:25.091
♪♪

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:26.893
-Thank you for finding the time,
Mr. Mayor!

00:00:26.893 --> 00:00:29.629
-Thank you, thank you.
It's good to be here.

00:00:29.629 --> 00:00:32.198
I'm normally up late at night,
leaving Zero Bond,

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:35.168
watching your show
before hanging out, you know.

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:36.502
-I don't know
when you're sleeping,

00:00:36.502 --> 00:00:38.871
because you are making
the rounds of this great city,

00:00:38.871 --> 00:00:42.208
and I think that's why people
are drawn to your term so far.

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:44.544
How are you finding it
in the early days?

00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:46.879
-This is an amazing place,
resilient.

00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:50.216
You know,
never bet against New York.

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:51.884
We --
-I think that's a very --

00:00:51.884 --> 00:00:53.386
-[ Cheers and applause ]
-We come back.

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:56.222
We come back stronger,
better than ever.

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:58.158
You know, we went through
through a difficult period

00:00:58.158 --> 00:01:00.960
during COVID,
but when you are out,

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.229
and New York is --
We're just ready.

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.165
The way goes New York
goes America.

00:01:05.165 --> 00:01:06.733
The way goes America
goes the globe.

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:08.067
It starts here in New York City.

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:09.568
-Well, that's very exciting.

00:01:09.568 --> 00:01:11.570
Are you reaching out

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:14.006
to any of the people
who've held the job previously?

00:01:14.006 --> 00:01:15.808
-Yes, all the time.

00:01:15.808 --> 00:01:18.745
You know, Mayor Bloomberg,
who is a good friend,

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:21.680
Mayor de Blasio, but also mayors
through the city.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.950
Mayor Suarez and I,
we're good friends.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:25.484
We're competitive.

00:01:25.484 --> 00:01:28.354
I'm saying to all those
New Yorkers who went to Miami...

00:01:28.354 --> 00:01:29.656
get your butt back to New York.

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:30.956
This is your city.

00:01:30.956 --> 00:01:33.525
-I think, you know, ultimately,
they're realizing

00:01:33.525 --> 00:01:35.328
they made a mistake
when they went down to Florida.

00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:36.629
It's not New York City.

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:38.898
-Listen.
You can only have so much sun.

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:40.766
You can only ride
up and down that beach

00:01:40.766 --> 00:01:41.934
every once in a while.

00:01:41.934 --> 00:01:43.102
New York, we have it all.

00:01:43.102 --> 00:01:44.303
-Yeah, we have it all.

00:01:44.303 --> 00:01:45.938
Now, maybe you can answer
a question for me.

00:01:45.938 --> 00:01:48.340
When does a person
officially become a New Yorker?

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:49.775
How long do you
have to live here?

00:01:49.775 --> 00:01:51.010
-Probably a combination.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:54.814
When you could
get on the subway station

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:56.615
and you get a seat
before anyone else,

00:01:56.615 --> 00:01:59.651
when you can navigate that,
I think that is --

00:01:59.651 --> 00:02:03.856
From day one, when you come
to New York and realize

00:02:03.856 --> 00:02:07.359
the power of our diversity,
that's our secret weapon.

00:02:07.359 --> 00:02:09.529
And if you are comfortable
in any neighborhood,

00:02:09.529 --> 00:02:11.697
you could walk into
a Chinese restaurant,

00:02:11.697 --> 00:02:15.067
have a Russian cook make you
an Italian dish that he learned

00:02:15.067 --> 00:02:18.404
from his Romanian girlfriend --
only in New York.

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:20.372
-Only in New York City
do you get all those things.

00:02:20.372 --> 00:02:21.741
You mentioned the subway.

00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:23.343
I will say, you know, obviously,

00:02:23.343 --> 00:02:25.344
this is a difficult time
for the subway.

00:02:25.344 --> 00:02:28.881
There's homelessness.
There are mental-health issues.

00:02:28.881 --> 00:02:31.183
I will also say
that I was very happy to see

00:02:31.183 --> 00:02:34.720
you and the governor addressing
this as a problem together.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:36.489
We've obviously gone through
a tough period of time

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:38.691
where the governor
and the mayor of this city

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:41.794
did not get along
particularly well.

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:44.296
Do you feel bullish about
the future of the subway system?

00:02:44.296 --> 00:02:45.865
Do you think
that you can make a change?

00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:48.935
And how do you deal
with the homelessness

00:02:48.935 --> 00:02:51.704
that is plaguing
the subway system

00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:53.439
in a way that's humane?
-A combination.

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:55.441
When I started
my law-enforcement career,

00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:58.477
I was a Transit police officer
during the '80s.

00:02:58.477 --> 00:03:01.614
We rode alone,
without a radio that operated.

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:03.149
It was something where you saw

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:05.684
graffiti everywhere,
crime, drugs.

00:03:05.684 --> 00:03:08.287
And we made that transformation.
We could do it again.

00:03:08.287 --> 00:03:11.090
But we have to do it
in a humane way.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:15.294
The subway system is a microcosm
of the pain people are feeling,

00:03:15.294 --> 00:03:17.964
and if we go down there
and just displace them

00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:19.766
without dealing with their pain,

00:03:19.766 --> 00:03:22.268
then we are just kicking the can
down the road.

00:03:22.268 --> 00:03:24.169
We have a comprehensive plan

00:03:24.169 --> 00:03:26.172
that is going to put people
in housing,

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:28.507
deal with
their mental-health issues,

00:03:28.507 --> 00:03:30.175
but also set the standard.

00:03:30.175 --> 00:03:34.614
You can't be on the train
shooting up drugs,

00:03:34.614 --> 00:03:38.384
pushing people on the tracks,
committing crimes, barbecuing.

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:40.853
No, this is
our transportation system!

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:42.254
-Yeah.
-And so we want to send

00:03:42.254 --> 00:03:44.991
the right message
that people should feel safe.

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:46.826
That's the lifeblood
of our city.

00:03:46.826 --> 00:03:48.962
If it's not safe --
It's the great equalizer,

00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:50.329
no matter how much you make.

00:03:50.329 --> 00:03:53.399
You could be from Wall Street
or live on my street,

00:03:53.399 --> 00:03:54.800
you take the subway system.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:57.503
We're all the same,
and I want a good, safe, clean,

00:03:57.503 --> 00:03:59.505
affordable, reliable
subway system.

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:01.507
And I'm going to give that
to New Yorkers.

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:03.075
-I think that's something
we could use.

00:04:03.075 --> 00:04:04.610
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.678
Two things I believe to be true.

00:04:06.678 --> 00:04:08.648
I believe this is
the greatest city in the world.

00:04:08.648 --> 00:04:10.015
I hope I live here forever.

00:04:10.015 --> 00:04:12.151
I believe there's way too much
garbage on the street.

00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:13.519
-Yes, yes.
-How does that --

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:15.154
You know,
there are obviously a lot

00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:18.257
of other cities in this country
that have large populations,

00:04:18.257 --> 00:04:20.827
not this big,
that do not have that problem.

00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:22.194
-You know, and you're right.

00:04:22.194 --> 00:04:25.865
And so what I have the team
doing, we're creating

00:04:25.865 --> 00:04:31.237
what we call an emerging-market
innovation hub in our city.

00:04:31.237 --> 00:04:33.172
And we want to go
all over the globe

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:35.574
and find out what are people
doing differently.

00:04:35.574 --> 00:04:38.077
So my goal is,
there's some places,

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:39.678
like Buenos Aires and others,

00:04:39.678 --> 00:04:41.881
where they're not putting
garbage on the street.

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:44.316
They put it into bins
at the end of corners

00:04:44.316 --> 00:04:45.685
so we could have clean streets.

00:04:45.685 --> 00:04:47.953
So we want to do things
differently.

00:04:47.953 --> 00:04:50.422
I don't want to think
outside the box.

00:04:50.422 --> 00:04:52.458
I want to destroy the darn box.

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:55.027
New York is supposed to
lead the way.

00:04:55.027 --> 00:04:57.330
And garbage is one way.
We don't want the rodents.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.031
I'm scared as hell
of those rats.

00:04:59.031 --> 00:05:00.566
-Yeah. This is a very --

00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:03.336
You're really facing your fears
by taking on this job, yeah.

00:05:03.336 --> 00:05:05.304
[ Laughter ]

00:05:05.304 --> 00:05:07.840
-So we want to deal with
the garbage on the street.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.278
The perception of filth,
of the street homelessness.

00:05:12.278 --> 00:05:14.813
We don't feel safe
in our city anymore,

00:05:14.813 --> 00:05:17.016
and trash plays a role in that.

00:05:17.016 --> 00:05:20.386
Cleanliness
is next to Godliness.

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:21.854
-That's what I keep hearing,

00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:24.123
and I think you're right
on that.

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:25.791
You're gonna be facing
some protesters

00:05:25.791 --> 00:05:27.326
on the steps
of City Hall tomorrow.

00:05:27.326 --> 00:05:29.662
-Yes.
-Some LGBTQ organizations

00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:31.664
are upset with
three appointments you made.

00:05:31.664 --> 00:05:33.999
These are people who have said
some pretty hateful things

00:05:33.999 --> 00:05:35.968
about gay people in the past.

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:37.403
They've apologized.

00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:39.705
Their apologies did coincide
with their appointments.

00:05:39.705 --> 00:05:42.975
And how do you --
What do you say to a community?

00:05:42.975 --> 00:05:45.244
And I should also note,
you've always been out in front.

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:46.479
You have a great record on this.

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:47.880
So, what do you say to people

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.315
who were disappointed
by those appointments?

00:05:49.315 --> 00:05:52.385
-This is such an important and
significant moment for our city,

00:05:52.385 --> 00:05:54.486
because -- you're right --
when you look at the record

00:05:54.486 --> 00:06:00.192
of making the important vote
for marriage, gender --

00:06:00.192 --> 00:06:02.428
even as a police captain

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:06.632
standing with members
of the LGBTQ-plus community.

00:06:06.632 --> 00:06:07.966
And so here we are.

00:06:07.966 --> 00:06:11.003
When I voted for marriage,
my brother-in-law told me,

00:06:11.003 --> 00:06:14.473
"You can never come back
in my church again."

00:06:14.473 --> 00:06:16.842
He later apologized for it
because, you know,

00:06:16.842 --> 00:06:19.579
he knew he was wrong,
and I spoke with him.

00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:23.416
We must move
from the cancel culture

00:06:23.416 --> 00:06:25.985
to the consultation culture.

00:06:25.985 --> 00:06:28.687
We have to take people --
Don't meet them where they are.

00:06:28.687 --> 00:06:29.955
See them where they are --

00:06:29.955 --> 00:06:31.323
Or, I should say,
meet them where they are

00:06:31.323 --> 00:06:32.925
and take them
where they ought to be.

00:06:32.925 --> 00:06:35.194
-[ Cheers and applause ]
-All of these --

00:06:35.194 --> 00:06:39.264
You know, people romanticize
now about marriage.

00:06:39.264 --> 00:06:41.767
But go back there
and look what America was.

00:06:41.767 --> 00:06:43.869
President Obama
was against marriage.

00:06:43.869 --> 00:06:46.105
Many of our elected officials
were against marriage.

00:06:46.105 --> 00:06:48.674
So when I was standing
on the Senate floor,

00:06:48.674 --> 00:06:51.143
saying that, no,
people have the right

00:06:51.143 --> 00:06:52.845
to marry the people they love,

00:06:52.845 --> 00:06:55.280
no matter
what their gender may be,

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:58.518
there were a lot of people
in America and New York

00:06:58.518 --> 00:07:00.619
who demonized folks like me.

00:07:00.619 --> 00:07:02.154
Now they came along.

00:07:02.154 --> 00:07:04.890
We should be in a position
of converting people

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:06.358
to be at the right place,

00:07:06.358 --> 00:07:08.127
'cause we got more battles
ahead of us.

00:07:08.127 --> 00:07:10.129
You know,
marriage was just one of them.

00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:14.734
And so I'm saying to the members
of the LGBT community,

00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:16.468
you're talking about Eric Adams.

00:07:16.468 --> 00:07:18.871
We fought together to get here.

00:07:18.871 --> 00:07:20.506
So you have
the right to protest.

00:07:20.506 --> 00:07:22.074
You're gonna come
inside City Hall

00:07:22.074 --> 00:07:24.243
and sit down and speak with me,
and we're gonna talk.

00:07:24.243 --> 00:07:26.678
But we're gonna go out there
and show people

00:07:26.678 --> 00:07:29.115
that we need to be
with those who are transgenders,

00:07:29.115 --> 00:07:30.883
because even
the transgenders believe

00:07:30.883 --> 00:07:34.453
that members of the community
have been mean to them.

00:07:34.453 --> 00:07:37.456
We're going to convert people
to be who we ought to be,

00:07:37.456 --> 00:07:40.226
not who they have been,
and that's what I'm going to do.

00:07:40.226 --> 00:07:42.027
-Well, I wish you
all the luck with that

00:07:42.027 --> 00:07:44.463
because I think that is
an important journey.

00:07:44.463 --> 00:07:47.099
You, um -- I want to end
by remarking on the fact

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:48.500
you're a very clean eater,

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:51.403
even though this is a hard city
to eat clean in.

00:07:51.403 --> 00:07:53.439
You're a vegan.
You eat a little fish.

00:07:53.439 --> 00:07:55.074
-No, I'm a plant-based eater.

00:07:55.074 --> 00:07:56.642
You know,
I want to keep my body tight.

00:07:56.642 --> 00:07:59.511
I got great abs.
I have a nice firm behind.

00:07:59.511 --> 00:08:01.013
-Everybody was saying that.
-I want to stay that way.

00:08:01.013 --> 00:08:02.214
-Everybody was saying that.

00:08:02.214 --> 00:08:03.849
You did a press conference
recently

00:08:03.849 --> 00:08:05.651
with a smoothie you drink
every day,

00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:07.119
and people remarked at the fact

00:08:07.119 --> 00:08:09.021
this did not look like
a delicious smoothie.

00:08:09.021 --> 00:08:11.524 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:08:13.392 --> 00:08:16.294 line:0%
How did it taste, Mayor?
And be honest.

00:08:16.294 --> 00:08:17.864 line:0%
-Listen, first of all,

00:08:17.864 --> 00:08:20.799 line:0%
don't look at the way
that it looks.

00:08:20.799 --> 00:08:24.937 line:0%
Blueberries are inside there,
cacao powder,

00:08:24.937 --> 00:08:29.208
matcha powder, spinach, kale.

00:08:29.208 --> 00:08:30.977
That's how I start my day,
every day.

00:08:30.977 --> 00:08:32.211
-Alright.

00:08:32.211 --> 00:08:34.613
-You know, and we want people
to enjoy --

00:08:34.613 --> 00:08:38.484
The fountain of youth
is in your food.

00:08:38.484 --> 00:08:40.452
And if you eat right,
you're going to look right

00:08:40.452 --> 00:08:43.022
and you're going to be right
to yourself and to others.

00:08:43.022 --> 00:08:44.390
-Alright, well, you know what?

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:46.291
I can't think of a better place
to leave it than that.

00:08:46.291 --> 00:08:47.759
Hey, thank you very much.

00:08:47.759 --> 00:08:49.528
That's Mayor Eric Adams,
everybody.

00:08:49.528 --> 00:08:51.564
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

